{
 "purdue": "Convert to accessible HTML compliant with WCAG 2.2 AA with complete MathJax headers and remove [cite].",
 "expert": "Convert the attached PDF (material for a STEM course) into ONE accessible HTML file for a blind student who uses a screen reader (NVDA, JAWS or VoiceOver). I will upload the file to our LMS (Canvas), which removes <script> elements, so the file must work with no JavaScript and no network access.\n\nRequirements:\n1. Fidelity. Transcribe ALL of the content in reading order, word for word. Do not summarise, shorten, paraphrase, merge, reorder or skip anything: keep every box, worked example, exercise, answer choice, caption, footnote and attribution line. Do not add anything that is not in the PDF (no new headings, introductions, notes, explanations or corrections). If the source contains an error, keep it exactly as written.\n2. Length. If the whole document does not fit in one reply, stop at the end of a complete section and write exactly CONTINUED IN NEXT REPLY. Never write \"...\", \"and so on\" or \"the rest follows the same pattern\".\n3. Structure. One <h1> for the document title, then <h2> to <h6> following the document's own heading hierarchy without skipping levels; real <ul>/<ol> lists; <table> only for real data tables, with a <caption>, <th scope=\"col\"> / <th scope=\"row\">, and rowspan/colspan for merged header cells. Keep equation numbers and cross-references (for example \"Equation (2.1)\", \"Figure 2.8\") exactly as they appear.\n4. Mathematics. Write every mathematical expression, inline or display, as presentation MathML (<math> elements; display=\"block\" for display equations) directly in the HTML. No MathJax, no LaTeX left in the text, no images of equations. Preserve the notation exactly: bold vectors (mathvariant=\"bold\"), arrows over letters, double-struck and script letters, signs, primes, subscripts, superscripts and units.\n5. Figures. You cannot embed the PDF's images, so for each figure write <figure><img src=\"figure-N.png\" alt=\"...\"><figcaption>...</figcaption></figure> with the PDF's own caption copied exactly, and alt text that describes only what is actually visible (no guessed details). For graphs and diagrams, add a longer description in a paragraph right after the figure. After the HTML, list the image files I must create (file name, PDF page, what to crop).\n6. <html lang=\"en\">, a <title>, UTF-8.\n7. Uncertainty. If any text, symbol or number is hard to read, give your best reading and mark it <mark>[CHECK: reason]</mark> so I can verify it.\n\nReturn the complete HTML in a single code block."
}
